                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[b]I have received this product for free, but it won't change my overall idea, nor that it will influence my opinion in any way.[/b]\n\n[i]TLDR; Look for ''Overall'' bellow[/i]\n\nPuzzle-Brawler does describe Fat Mask pretty well.\nLet me summarize: There is 2 main game mode: Solo/Coop and Free for all.\nIn the solo/coop mode, you have to build block combos before a time limit (represented by 2 red ''walls'' that close on the screen. Once they touch it's time out). As soon as you do a combo, the ''walls'' withdraw themselves a bit. The greater the combo is, the wider the walls will open. This can be played with friends: Building the biggest combo using teamwork.\n\nThe Free-for-All mode is a 2-4 players brawler in which you have to do combos and ''close'' them. In order to do so, once the player has hitted 3 or more blocks of the same color, the combo will have a timer before it automatically closes, becoming yours and adding the score of the combo to your total score. You can either expand the combo (adding blocks) and resetting the combo timer or close it by throwing a block to the ''target'' block. The thing is, before closing a combo, anyone can add a block onto it and/or close it. It means that anybody can take your points away, taking your hard work away from you.\n\nI will break down this review into 4 main points: Gameplay, Music, Graphics, Fun Factor.(yes!).\n\n[b]Gameplay:[/b]\nThe thing that is original about FatMask is that it's not your typical puzzle game. Instead of moving the blocks like you would do with tetris, Candy Crush Saga, Tricky Tower, Dr. Mario or Columns, you have a character to move in an arena. This character îs used to pick and throw blocks in order to create color-based combos. You pick and throw blocks into one another to create a chain of 3 colors, then you can close the combo or expend it by adding another block to it.\n\nThe mechanics are simple but get deeper once you master the basics. So: If two combos of different colors touch each other, they become a greater combo. If you create a combo on a target block (opening another combo by throwing a block onto the target block of another combo) it closes both combos and fuses them. It's a little har to explain at first\n\nControls are tight and simple to learn. You use D-Pad/Jostick/WASD to move, one button to take and throw blocks, one button to jump and one to fart (giving a little speed boost, but i am not sure if it is useful or not TBH). \nThe only negative point is that it seems that sometimes I wanted to throw a block upward (pressing up) and the character threw it downward instead but I don't know if in the heat of the fight I pressed the wrong direction. It might've happened since that this is kind of a fast paced game.\n\n[b]Graphics[/b]\n[i] YOU CAN'T GET LOST [/i]. Let me explain that. The game is beautifully colored and colors are varied. Every type of block has it's own color and it's own shape. Every character has it's own color. The character's colors are distinct from the blocks colors. In other words you can't get lost on the screen. Even in the madness that is the 4-players mode, you always know where you are and where the open combos are. In my opinion, this is genius. In one look you know what happens and where you are.\n\nThe background is also very distinct from the foreground. And the blend of color is easy on the eyes and pretty. It is also different than the 8-bit 16-bit trend that there is in the indi world right now making the art really unique.\n\n[b]Music[/b]\nAntoine Vachon did an incredible job on the songs. They all feel unique and gives the game just the right touch. If you haven't listened to it yet, it is available on his soundcloud. Even if it is a great ''combo'' with the game (bad pun intended) it's also enjoyable on it's own.\n\n[b]Fun Factor[/b]\nThe game is fun. It is fun if it's your first time playing it, and it's still fun to play after putting in hours into it (it's written that I've played 0.8hrs but i've played a lot more actually). It's best played with friends, but the solo mode is also enjoyable. I would easily play this during a whole night with friends.\n\nAlso this is a couch multiplayer game there is no online gameplay.\n\n[b]Overall[/b]\nThis game is worth it's 9.99 USD price tag. It is fun to play with friends and family (if you don't mind breaking up friendships). I recommend it for all it's qualities I've listed above. It is worth adding to your party nights along Towerfall, Tricky Tower and other party games!\n\n[b]So sit down on your couch, grab your controllers, some chips and diet coke and have fun [/b]\n[b]9.2/10[/b]",
